  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how engineered cell therapies can be programmed with synthetic gene circuits to improve specificity and function. In the upper part, a CAR-T cell is shown integrating multiple inputs from a tumor cell. Tumor surface antigens provide activating signals, while a “safety” small molecule can impose additional control. These inputs feed into logic-like rules, illustrated as “AND” and “NOT,” so the cell kills a tumor only when the right combination of cues is present and inappropriate cues are absent. The smaller schematics on the right suggest two ways this control can be implemented: direct CAR recognition of one antigen linked to an output, or a more layered design in which a synNotch receptor sensing antigen A induces a second receptor or module that then responds to antigen B.\n\nThe lower part extends this idea beyond killing. Here, engineered cells first sense internal or environmental regulatory factors and then trigger a chosen response, depicted as secretion. The example on the right shows inflammatory signaling being redirected so that detection of an inflammatory cue leads to production of an anti-inflammatory cytokine. Overall, the figure conveys programmable immune-cell behavior through modular sensing, logic gating, and tailored outputs.",
